{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q34iUdvqbqShLi1WdKYdFU","type":"tool_result","content":"SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean\nBitProgram.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean\nSqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean\nSqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean\n===== printaxioms\n17\n35\n===== closure-only\nclosure files with #print axioms 8 lines 19\n===== lakefile hooks\n\n-- Configuration elaboration precedes dependency resolution, unlike `post_update`.\nrun_cmd do\n  let env ← Lean.getEnv\n  if let some root := Lake.dirExt.getState env then\n    let options ← Lean.getOptions\n    let changed ← Lean.Elab.Command.liftIO do\n      let declarations ← IO.ofExcept <| Lake.packageDepAttr.getAllEntries env |>.mapM fun name =>\n        unsafe env.evalConstCheck Dependency options ``Dependency name\n      let mut dependencies := #[]\n      for name in preResolutionPatchNames do\n        let some dependency := declarations.find? (·.name == name)\n          | throw <| IO.userError s!\"Missing direct dependency declaration for {name}.\"\n        dependencies := dependencies.push dependency\n      preparePatchedDependencies root dependencies\n    for label in changed do\n      Lean.logInfo s!\"{label}: applied compatibility patch before dependency resolution\"\n\npost_update pkg do\n...\npost_update pkg do\n  for name in preResolutionPatchNames do\n    let label := name.toString (escape := false)\n    let some dependency ← findPackageByName? name\n      | error s!\"Missing dependency {label} after Lake update.\"\n    let some declaration := pkg.depConfigs.find? (·.name == name)\n      | error s!\"Missing direct dependency declaration for {label}.\"\n...\n===== preprint\n25:\\date{17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026}\n7:**Formalization Fix**\n18:- *The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.* The paper gives a deterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of fixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The case of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent working group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo, Ramamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof. At the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so others can examine them as well.\n# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n\nThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n\n- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n\n## Scope\n\nFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n\nFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n\n## Comparator links\n\n| Result | Comparator statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |\n===== refs\n10:@misc{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026,\n19:@article{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001,\n29:@article{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001,\n39:@misc{SienickiSienicki2026,\n54:@misc{LeanComparator,\n65:@misc{Nanoda,\n===== sec3 head\n% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and\n% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the\n% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with\n% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is\n% checked and the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\\sloppy\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three 