{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgj5UJsGMiGP8y9sBEhez","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01L4MBXLCE1Hbo9tLp53f6Sn","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex","content":"% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04 (2026-11-05). Every\n% number of this section is printed, and every line citation re-checked\n% against the read-only clone at commit 4eaee875, by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py\n% which re-runs the two audit scripts of reports/scratch/eth_audit/\n% (closure.py, defdiff.py) as subprocesses, performs the remaining checks\n% itself, writes latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex, and exits non-zero if\n% anything has moved. Unqualified Lean paths below are relative to\n% lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/ in the clone; the challenge file is\n% lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean. Nothing in the clone was\n% built, imported or run for this section; the build is Section 5's.\n\\section{The proof, inspected}\n\\label{sec:proof}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} read the theorem; this section reads the\nproof, as far as reading can go. The question is not whether the\nmathematics is right---the kernel decides that, and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nreports what the kernel said when we ran it---but whether anything in the\nsource could make the kernel's acceptance mean less than it appears to\nmean: an axiom, a \\texttt{sorry}, a trusted evaluation, an unsound\ninstance, a patched Mathlib, a statement that differs from the challenge's,\nor a cost model of the proof's own that leaks into the theorem. We found\nnone of these. What we did find is that the repository offers no recorded\nevidence that the closure type-checks at all---no \\texttt{\\#print axioms}\noutput, no continuous integration, no second kernel for this family---which\nis why Section~\\ref{sec:replay} exists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n\\subsection{Method}\n\\label{sec:proof-method}\n\nThe clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated as\nuntrusted data: every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},\n\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,\nimported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of\n2026-11-05 do the mechanical part: \\texttt{closure.py} follows\n\\texttt{import} lines from the solution module, strips comments (nested\nblock comments included) and counts keyword occurrences in what remains;\n\\texttt{defdiff.py} normalises whitespace and compares each declaration of\nthe challenge file with the library's declaration of the same name. Both\nwere re-run for this section, and every number below is from that fresh\nrun; \\repofile{paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py} re-runs them, performs\nthe remaining checks, and regenerates Table~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection}.\nThe inspection is of the spine of the proof---the final file, the\ncompiler, the entry lemmas, the constants---and of the whole closure by\nkeyword. The arithmetic beneath the spine was not read\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof-not}).\n\n\\subsection{The closure}\n\\label{sec:proof-closure}\n\nThe comparator's JSON names one solution module,\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, a sixteen-line aggregator\nthat imports \\texttt{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and\n\\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing\n(\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its import closure inside the repository is\n\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one with the prefix\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}, totalling \\textbf{550,551} raw\nlines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}). The\nclosure stops at \\textbf{50} Mathlib modules and one module of Lean's own\nlibrary, \\texttt{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega}, imported by\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean} (line~14) and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean} (line~5); it\nis a tactic module, and the closure defines no tactic, macro or syntax of\nits own (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}). Nothing is imported from any\nother family of the collection or from any of the repository's other\ndependencies.\n\nThe family's directory holds \\textbf{3,977} \\texttt{.lean} files, so\n\\textbf{886} are outside the closure. They are not reachable from the\ncollection's root module either: \\clonefile{lean/OAI.lean} imports the\nfamily only at its line~49, \\texttt{import\nOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, and the lakefile's default target\nis that root (\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~89,\n\\texttt{@[default\\_target] lean\\_lib OAI}). They are therefore never built\nby the default target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For\ncompleteness, a raw search of the 886 for \\texttt{sorry}, \\texttt{axiom},\n\\texttt{native\\_decide} and \\texttt{admit} finds nothing, with or without\ncomments.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep}\n\\label{sec:proof-keywords}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection} gives the counts over the 3,091 files\nwith comments stripped. The ones that matter are the zeros. There is no\n\\texttt{axiom}, no \\texttt{sorry}, no \\texttt{admit}; no\n\\texttt{native\\_decide} and no \\texttt{ofReduceBool}, so nothing is\nproved by trusting the compiled evaluator; no \\texttt{implemented\\_by},\n\\texttt{extern}, \\texttt{unsafe} or \\texttt{partial}, so no definition\nhas a hidden implementation and every recursion is one the kernel checks\n(nine \\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses);\nno \\texttt{elab}, \\texttt{macro}, \\texttt{syntax} or \\texttt{simproc}, so\nthere is no metaprogramming that could rewrite goals outside the kernel's\nview. The only \\texttt{set\\_option}s are two\n\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),\nwhich switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9).\n\nNine declarations are \\texttt{opaque}. Every one carries a body: three\nrecovery functions (\\texttt{queryRecovery},\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean} line~59 with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery},\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean} lines 178 and\n198) and six \\texttt{private opaque} constants of subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = …\\}} with an explicit value\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} line~22 and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding\nis hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the\ntype, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}\ndeclarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit\nuses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the\nclassical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s\nand 40 \\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype},\n\\texttt{Decidable} and algebraic instances; an instance cannot be\n``inconsistent'' without an axiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines\nwith \\texttt{decide} are kernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the\nnative evaluator.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The inspection in numbers. Comments stripped where a keyword is\ncounted. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof\\_inspection.py}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-inspection}\n\\input{tables/proof_inspection}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The statement is the challenge's}\n\\label{sec:proof-match}\n\nThe solution file does not import the challenge file:\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins (lines 1--3) with\n\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement} and\n\\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names,\nspread over seven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the\nbit arithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding\nfunctions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in\n\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the\nspace instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and\n\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}\n(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares\n\\textbf{35} declarations: \\textbf{33} are identical character for\ncharacter after whitespace normalisation, and the remaining two,\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} and \\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the\nchallenge writes \\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15)---a difference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement\nitself, from \\texttt{theorem exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by},\nis \\textbf{identical} in the two files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124\nafter normalisation, at line~194 of the challenge and line~74 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the challenge's proof is the single\nword \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\n\nThis textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The\ncomparator \\citep{LeanComparator} compares the two statements at the\nlevel of exported constants, with every declaration the statement\nmentions matched, so a library definition that differed from the\nchallenge's under the same name would make the comparator fail rather\nthan pass silently. What the text confirms is that there is no such\ndifference to find, and that the shape Section~\\ref{sec:statement} read\nis the shape the library proves.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof}\n\\label{sec:proof-spine}\n\n\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is\n\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape\n\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k (NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing, and it is the\nreason no running time can be read off the statement\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\n\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},\n\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nlines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with \\texttt{1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A}\n(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract\n(\\texttt{NativeLeafContract.uniform\\_contract}, line~39; the contract\ngives \\texttt{1 ≤ At}, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\n(\\texttt{Filter.eventually\\_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$\nfactor is assembled by \\texttt{runtime\\_of\\_tree\\_and\\_leaf} (line~46)\nfrom the DFS visit bound of \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most\na square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)\nand the leaf time. For \\texttt{n ≤ cutoff},\n\\texttt{Native.entry\\_small\\_primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives\n\\texttt{time ≤ 28700 · (L + 1)²} (line~92) through the finite scanner\nof \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are\nfolded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and\n\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: \\texttt{db +\n(4 · (k − 2) + 2) + df + 2}) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} said from the statement side: the constants\nare unknown, and the \\emph{smallest} they could be is already far from\n$1$.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nA proof of a running-time bound for a machine it compiles from a\nhigher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound against a cost\nfunction of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function's\narithmetic. That is not what happens here. The compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive\\_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\texttt{CompileSpace.compile\\_word\\_answer\\_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time …} with\n\\texttt{time ≤ 7 * sourceSteps} and\n\\texttt{visitedSpace intermediate ≤ s * (bound + input.length + 15)} on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\texttt{primitive\\_located\\_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are\nthe challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and\n\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse. This is the structural point on which the rest of the paper\nleans: whatever the proof's internal accounting, the number the theorem\nbounds is the number of steps of the comparator's machine.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\texttt{Native.counting\\_primitive\\_from\\_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement. These are theorems about the concrete step function.\nThe arithmetic that makes them true---sparsification, the ring and\nmoment computations, the DFS---is the content of the other three\nthousand files, and the only claim made about it here is the one the\nkernel makes: it elaborates (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks}\n\\label{sec:proof-build}\n\nThe toolchain is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:\n\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}\nline~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest lists 42\npackages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at\n\\texttt{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05\nconfirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that bumps the\ntoolchain to \\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility\npatches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/}, \\textbf{none for Mathlib};\nevery one is for a third-party package (\\texttt{AINTLIB},\n\\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd}, \\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, …) that\nthis closure does not import. The lakefile has two hooks that run\narbitrary IO when Lake configures or updates the package: a\n\\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that evaluates dependency\ndeclarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck} and calls\n\\texttt{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches eleven\ndependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward) that\nverifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only\n\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages; they are one\nreason the inspection did not build, and the reason the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n\\subsection{What the repository does not record}\n\\label{sec:proof-missing}\n\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a\nforty-year-old hypothesis are absent, and their absence is a fact about\nevidence rather than about the proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No axiom audit.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} ends\n  with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and seven\n  other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen commands in\n  eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such files, but\n  nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output exists only\n  while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of the whole\n  clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{No continuous integration.} \\clonefile{.github/workflows/}\n  holds one file, \\texttt{lock-prs.yml}, which locks pull-request\n  conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\\item \\textbf{No second kernel for this family.} The comparator's JSON\n  (\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}) names the\n  one theorem, an empty \\texttt{definition\\_names}, the three permitted\n  axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice},\n  and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}; the registry\n  \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines 1683--1685) points at the\n  same declaration and file. Of the collection's 418 challenge JSONs,\n  413 set \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda} to \\texttt{false}, two to\n  \\texttt{true}, and three omit the key. With it off, the collection's own\n  check of this family rests on one kernel implementation. The\n  comparator's README, as fetched by the audit, describes the check as:\n  build challenge and solution in a sandbox, export both with\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, match each named theorem's statement with every\n  declaration it uses, confine the proof to the permitted axioms, and\n  have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on, the exported\n  environment is replayed through an independent kernel in Rust as well\n  \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the versions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nTwo smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source is\ndated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''\n(\\clonefile{preprints/…/build/main\\_full\\_version.tex}, line~25); the\ncollection's history file mentions the family once, in the sentence that\nnames the working group at whose request the materials were released\n(\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only ``Formalization Fix''\nentry (line~7) concerns another family. And the scope note\n\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as\n$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library\nproves and what Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} compares with the manuscript's.\n\n\\subsection{The trust boundary, joined with the replay}\n\\label{sec:proof-trust}\n\nThe audit's verdict listed what the proof's trust rested on before\nanything was run: (i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed\nthe closure type-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output;\n(iii) the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7}\nas upstream; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no second\nkernel for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which of the\nfive Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The trust boundary before and after the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-trust}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\\linewidth}p{0.64\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\nitem & status after the replay \\\\\n\\midrule\n(i) the build & \\textbf{moved}: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox at the pinned toolchain, exit~0, no \\texttt{error}, no \\texttt{sorry}. \\\\\n(ii) the axiom output & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the comparator's theorem printed \\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, and the nineteen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record. \\\\\n(iii) kernel and Mathlib & \\textbf{moved in part}: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned revision and not patched, so ``as upstream'' is now observed rather than assumed; the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} is still trusted as upstream, now beside a second kernel (v). \\\\\n(iv) the comparator run & \\textbf{not moved}: the statement-level match through \\texttt{lean4export} was not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof-match}. \\\\\n(v) a second kernel & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} checked the exported dependencies of the theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms; what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on: the Lean kernel of\n\\texttt{v4.34.1} \\emph{and} nanoda agreeing on the exported dependencies\nof the theorem; \\texttt{lean4export} exporting what the environment\nholds; upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not\nexport-level, match between the solution's statement and the\nchallenge's. Everything the inspection could find by reading, the build\nand the two kernels confirmed; nothing the inspection could not settle\nby reading has been settled by anything else.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:proof-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{The arithmetic was not read.} The inspection followed the\n  spine from \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} down to the compile\n  lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the sparsification, ring\n  and DFS content of the other three thousand files was not read. The\n  argument above is that its correctness is kernel-enforced, not that we\n  verified it.\n\\item \\textbf{Grep is necessary, not sufficient.} Zero \\texttt{sorry}\n  and zero \\texttt{axiom} by text search cannot show that a file\n  elaborates; only the build does (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Conversely\n  a keyword count is over source text at one commit, and a declaration\n  can be unsound only through the mechanisms searched for; we searched\n  for the ones we know.\n\\item \\textbf{The textual statement match is not the comparator's.} The\n  comparator matches exported constants; we matched source text after\n  whitespace normalisation and found it identical. A difference the\n  normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well,\n  but that is an argument, not a run.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the constants' values.} The bounds\n  $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ are what the proof's witnesses are\n  built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n  statements about any machine's running time, and a different proof of\n  the same statement could exhibit different ones.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain.}\n  All three are trusted as upstream at the pinned versions; the second\n  kernel of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is evidence about the first, not a\n  proof of either.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the theorem says.} That is\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}; a sound proof of a weak bound is a sound\n  proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4644,"cache_read_input_tokens":165817,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4644,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a784a7f7-656a-4734-be78-2354d2f491db","uuid":"91e651d5-999a-4301-9e82-a9ef03f5d6bc","timestamp":"2026-11-05T03:35:34.348Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj5UJYf7sYBEdW82pQXT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01L4MBXLCE1Hbo9tLp53f6Sn":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex","content":"% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04 (2026-11-05). Every\n% number of this section is printed, and every line citation re-checked\n% against the read-only clone at commit 4eaee875, by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py\n% which re-runs the two audit scripts of reports/scratch/eth_audit/\n% (closure.py, defdiff.py) as subprocesses, performs the remaining checks\n% itself, writes latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex, and exits non-zero if\n% anything has moved. Unqualified Lean paths below are relative to\n% lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/ in the clone; the challenge file is\n% lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean. Nothing in the clone was\n% built, imported or run for this section; the build is Section 5's.\n\\section{The proof, inspected}\n\\label{sec:proof}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} read the theorem; this section reads the\nproof, as far as reading can go. The question is not whether the\nmathematics is right---the kernel decides that, and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nreports what the kernel said when we ran it---but whether anything in the\nsource could make the kernel's acceptance mean less than it appears to\nmean: an axiom, a \\texttt{sorry}, a trusted evaluation, an unsound\ninstance, a patched Mathlib, a statement that differs from the challenge's,\nor a cost model of the proof's own that leaks into the theorem. We found\nnone of these. What we did find is that the repository offers no recorded\nevidence that the closure type-checks at all---no \\texttt{\\#print axioms}\noutput, no continuous integration, no second kernel for this family---which\nis why Section~\\ref{sec:replay} exists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n\\subsection{Method}\n\\label{sec:proof-method}\n\nThe clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated as\nuntrusted data: every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},\n\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,\nimported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of\n2026-11-05 do the mechanical part: \\texttt{closure.py} follows\n\\texttt{import} lines from the solution module, strips comments (nested\nblock comments included) and counts keyword occurrences in what remains;\n\\texttt{defdiff.py} normalises whitespace and compares each declaration of\nthe challenge file with the library's declaration of the same name. Both\nwere re-run for this section, and every number below is from that fresh\nrun; \\repofile{paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py} re-runs them, performs\nthe remaining checks, and regenerates Table~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection}.\nThe inspection is of the spine of the proof---the final file, the\ncompiler, the entry lemmas, the constants---and of the whole closure by\nkeyword. The arithmetic beneath the spine was not read\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof-not}).\n\n\\subsection{The closure}\n\\label{sec:proof-closure}\n\nThe comparator's JSON names one solution module,\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, a sixteen-line aggregator\nthat imports \\texttt{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and\n\\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing\n(\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its import closure inside the repository is\n\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one with the prefix\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}, totalling \\textbf{550,551} raw\nlines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}). The\nclosure stops at \\textbf{50} Mathlib modules and one module of Lean's own\nlibrary, \\texttt{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega}, imported by\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean} (line~14) and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean} (line~5); it\nis a tactic module, and the closure defines no tactic, macro or syntax of\nits own (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}). Nothing is imported from any\nother family of the collection or from any of the repository's other\ndependencies.\n\nThe family's directory holds \\textbf{3,977} \\texttt{.lean} files, so\n\\textbf{886} are outside the closure. They are not reachable from the\ncollection's root module either: \\clonefile{lean/OAI.lean} imports the\nfamily only at its line~49, \\texttt{import\nOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, and the lakefile's default target\nis that root (\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~89,\n\\texttt{@[default\\_target] lean\\_lib OAI}). They are therefore never built\nby the default target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For\ncompleteness, a raw search of the 886 for \\texttt{sorry}, \\texttt{axiom},\n\\texttt{native\\_decide} and \\texttt{admit} finds nothing, with or without\ncomments.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep}\n\\label{sec:proof-keywords}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection} gives the counts over the 3,091 files\nwith comments stripped. The ones that matter are the zeros. There is no\n\\texttt{axiom}, no \\texttt{sorry}, no \\texttt{admit}; no\n\\texttt{native\\_decide} and no \\texttt{ofReduceBool}, so nothing is\nproved by trusting the compiled evaluator; no \\texttt{implemented\\_by},\n\\texttt{extern}, \\texttt{unsafe} or \\texttt{partial}, so no definition\nhas a hidden implementation and every recursion is one the kernel checks\n(nine \\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses);\nno \\texttt{elab}, \\texttt{macro}, \\texttt{syntax} or \\texttt{simproc}, so\nthere is no metaprogramming that could rewrite goals outside the kernel's\nview. The only \\texttt{set\\_option}s are two\n\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),\nwhich switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9).\n\nNine declarations are \\texttt{opaque}. Every one carries a body: three\nrecovery functions (\\texttt{queryRecovery},\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean} line~59 with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery},\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean} lines 178 and\n198) and six \\texttt{private opaque} constants of subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = …\\}} with an explicit value\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} line~22 and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding\nis hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the\ntype, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}\ndeclarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit\nuses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the\nclassical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s\nand 40 \\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype},\n\\texttt{Decidable} and algebraic instances; an instance cannot be\n``inconsistent'' without an axiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines\nwith \\texttt{decide} are kernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the\nnative evaluator.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The inspection in numbers. Comments stripped where a keyword is\ncounted. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof\\_inspection.py}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-inspection}\n\\input{tables/proof_inspection}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The statement is the challenge's}\n\\label{sec:proof-match}\n\nThe solution file does not import the challenge file:\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins (lines 1--3) with\n\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement} and\n\\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names,\nspread over seven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the\nbit arithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding\nfunctions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in\n\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the\nspace instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and\n\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}\n(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares\n\\textbf{35} declarations: \\textbf{33} are identical character for\ncharacter after whitespace normalisation, and the remaining two,\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} and \\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the\nchallenge writes \\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15)---a difference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement\nitself, from \\texttt{theorem exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by},\nis \\textbf{identical} in the two files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124\nafter normalisation, at line~194 of the challenge and line~74 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the challenge's proof is the single\nword \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\n\nThis textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The\ncomparator \\citep{LeanComparator} compares the two statements at the\nlevel of exported constants, with every declaration the statement\nmentions matched, so a library definition that differed from the\nchallenge's under the same name would make the comparator fail rather\nthan pass silently. What the text confirms is that there is no such\ndifference to find, and that the shape Section~\\ref{sec:statement} read\nis the shape the library proves.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof}\n\\label{sec:proof-spine}\n\n\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is\n\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape\n\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k (NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing, and it is the\nreason no running time can be read off the statement\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\n\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},\n\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nlines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with \\texttt{1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A}\n(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract\n(\\texttt{NativeLeafContract.uniform\\_contract}, line~39; the contract\ngives \\texttt{1 ≤ At}, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\n(\\texttt{Filter.eventually\\_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$\nfactor is assembled by \\texttt{runtime\\_of\\_tree\\_and\\_leaf} (line~46)\nfrom the DFS visit bound of \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most\na square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)\nand the leaf time. For \\texttt{n ≤ cutoff},\n\\texttt{Native.entry\\_small\\_primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives\n\\texttt{time ≤ 28700 · (L + 1)²} (line~92) through the finite scanner\nof \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are\nfolded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and\n\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: \\texttt{db +\n(4 · (k − 2) + 2) + df + 2}) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} said from the statement side: the constants\nare unknown, and the \\emph{smallest} they could be is already far from\n$1$.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nA proof of a running-time bound for a machine it compiles from a\nhigher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound against a cost\nfunction of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function's\narithmetic. That is not what happens here. The compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive\\_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\texttt{CompileSpace.compile\\_word\\_answer\\_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time …} with\n\\texttt{time ≤ 7 * sourceSteps} and\n\\texttt{visitedSpace intermediate ≤ s * (bound + input.length + 15)} on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\texttt{primitive\\_located\\_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are\nthe challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and\n\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse. This is the structural point on which the rest of the paper\nleans: whatever the proof's internal accounting, the number the theorem\nbounds is the number of steps of the comparator's machine.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\texttt{Native.counting\\_primitive\\_from\\_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement. These are theorems about the concrete step function.\nThe arithmetic that makes them true---sparsification, the ring and\nmoment computations, the DFS---is the content of the other three\nthousand files, and the only claim made about it here is the one the\nkernel makes: it elaborates (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks}\n\\label{sec:proof-build}\n\nThe toolchain is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:\n\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}\nline~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest lists 42\npackages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at\n\\texttt{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05\nconfirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that bumps the\ntoolchain to \\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility\npatches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/}, \\textbf{none for Mathlib};\nevery one is for a third-party package (\\texttt{AINTLIB},\n\\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd}, \\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, …) that\nthis closure does not import. The lakefile has two hooks that run\narbitrary IO when Lake configures or updates the package: a\n\\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that evaluates dependency\ndeclarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck} and calls\n\\texttt{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches eleven\ndependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward) that\nverifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only\n\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages; they are one\nreason the inspection did not build, and the reason the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n\\subsection{What the repository does not record}\n\\label{sec:proof-missing}\n\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a\nforty-year-old hypothesis are absent, and their absence is a fact about\nevidence rather than about the proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No axiom audit.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} ends\n  with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and seven\n  other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen commands in\n  eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such files, but\n  nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output exists only\n  while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of the whole\n  clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{No continuous integration.} \\clonefile{.github/workflows/}\n  holds one file, \\texttt{lock-prs.yml}, which locks pull-request\n  conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\\item \\textbf{No second kernel for this family.} The comparator's JSON\n  (\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}) names the\n  one theorem, an empty \\texttt{definition\\_names}, the three permitted\n  axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice},\n  and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}; the registry\n  \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines 1683--1685) points at the\n  same declaration and file. Of the collection's 418 challenge JSONs,\n  413 set \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda} to \\texttt{false}, two to\n  \\texttt{true}, and three omit the key. With it off, the collection's own\n  check of this family rests on one kernel implementation. The\n  comparator's README, as fetched by the audit, describes the check as:\n  build challenge and solution in a sandbox, export both with\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, match each named theorem's statement with every\n  declaration it uses, confine the proof to the permitted axioms, and\n  have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on, the exported\n  environment is replayed through an independent kernel in Rust as well\n  \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the versions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nTwo smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source is\ndated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''\n(\\clonefile{preprints/…/build/main\\_full\\_version.tex}, line~25); the\ncollection's history file mentions the family once, in the sentence that\nnames the working group at whose request the materials were released\n(\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only ``Formalization Fix''\nentry (line~7) concerns another family. And the scope note\n\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as\n$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library\nproves and what Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} compares with the manuscript's.\n\n\\subsection{The trust boundary, joined with the replay}\n\\label{sec:proof-trust}\n\nThe audit's verdict listed what the proof's trust rested on before\nanything was run: (i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed\nthe closure type-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output;\n(iii) the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7}\nas upstream; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no second\nkernel for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which of the\nfive Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The trust boundary before and after the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-trust}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\\linewidth}p{0.64\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\nitem & status after the replay \\\\\n\\midrule\n(i) the build & \\textbf{moved}: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox at the pinned toolchain, exit~0, no \\texttt{error}, no \\texttt{sorry}. \\\\\n(ii) the axiom output & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the comparator's theorem printed \\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, and the nineteen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record. \\\\\n(iii) kernel and Mathlib & \\textbf{moved in part}: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned revision and not patched, so ``as upstream'' is now observed rather than assumed; the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} is still trusted as upstream, now beside a second kernel (v). \\\\\n(iv) the comparator run & \\textbf{not moved}: the statement-level match through \\texttt{lean4export} was not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof-match}. \\\\\n(v) a second kernel & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} checked the exported dependencies of the theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms; what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on: the Lean kernel of\n\\texttt{v4.34.1} \\emph{and} nanoda agreeing on the exported dependencies\nof the theorem; \\texttt{lean4export} exporting what the environment\nholds; upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not\nexport-level, match between the solution's statement and the\nchallenge's. Everything the inspection could find by reading, the build\nand the two kernels confirmed; nothing the inspection could not settle\nby reading has been settled by anything else.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:proof-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{The arithmetic was not read.} The inspection followed the\n  spine from \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} down to the compile\n  lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the sparsification, ring\n  and DFS content of the other three thousand files was not read. The\n  argument above is that its correctness is kernel-enforced, not that we\n  verified it.\n\\item \\textbf{Grep is necessary, not sufficient.} Zero \\texttt{sorry}\n  and zero \\texttt{axiom} by text search cannot show that a file\n  elaborates; only the build does (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Conversely\n  a keyword count is over source text at one commit, and a declaration\n  can be unsound only through the mechanisms searched for; we searched\n  for the ones we know.\n\\item \\textbf{The textual statement match is not the comparator's.} The\n  comparator matches exported constants; we matched source text after\n  whitespace normalisation and found it identical. A difference the\n  normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well,\n  but that is an argument, not a run.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the constants' values.} The bounds\n  $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ are what the proof's witnesses are\n  built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n  statements about any machine's running time, and a different proof of\n  the same statement could exhibit different ones.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain.}\n  All three are trusted as upstream at the pinned versions; the second\n  kernel of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is evidence about the first, not a\n  proof of either.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the theorem says.} That is\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}; a sound proof of a weak bound is a sound\n  proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L4MBXLCE1Hbo9tLp53f6Sn","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a784a7f7-656a-4734-be78-2354d2f491db","uuid":"676b844e-c1ed-4e84-b3ab-1cf90c30906e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T03:35:34.367Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec4_proof.tex","content":"% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04 (2026-11-05). Every\n% number of this section is printed, and every line citation re-checked\n% against the read-only clone at commit 4eaee875, by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py\n% which re-runs the two audit scripts of reports/scratch/eth_audit/\n% (closure.py, defdiff.py) as subprocesses, performs the remaining checks\n% itself, writes latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex, and exits non-zero if\n% anything has moved. Unqualified Lean paths below are relative to\n% lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/ in the clone; the challenge file is\n% lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean. Nothing in the clone was\n% built, imported or run for this section; the build is Section 5's.\n\\section{The proof, inspected}\n\\label{sec:proof}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} read the theorem; this section reads the\nproof, as far as reading can go. The question is not whether the\nmathematics is right---the kernel decides that, and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nreports what the kernel said when we ran it---but whether anything in the\nsource could make the kernel's acceptance mean less than it appears to\nmean: an axiom, a \\texttt{sorry}, a trusted evaluation, an unsound\ninstance, a patched Mathlib, a statement that differs from the challenge's,\nor a cost model of the proof's own that leaks into the theorem. We found\nnone of these. What we did find is that the repository offers no recorded\nevidence that the closure type-checks at all---no \\texttt{\\#print axioms}\noutput, no continuous integration, no second kernel for this family---which\nis why Section~\\ref{sec:replay} exists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n\\subsection{Method}\n\\label{sec:proof-method}\n\nThe clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated as\nuntrusted data: every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},\n\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,\nimported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of\n2026-11-05 do the mechanical part: \\texttt{closure.py} follows\n\\texttt{import} lines from the solution module, strips comments (nested\nblock comments included) and counts keyword occurrences in what remains;\n\\texttt{defdiff.py} normalises whitespace and compares each declaration of\nthe challenge file with the library's declaration of the same name. Both\nwere re-run for this section, and every number below is from that fresh\nrun; \\repofile{paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py} re-runs them, performs\nthe remaining checks, and regenerates Table~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection}.\nThe inspection is of the spine of the proof---the final file, the\ncompiler, the entry lemmas, the constants---and of the whole closure by\nkeyword. The arithmetic beneath the spine was not read\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof-not}).\n\n\\subsection{The closure}\n\\label{sec:proof-closure}\n\nThe comparator's JSON names one solution module,\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, a sixteen-line aggregator\nthat imports \\texttt{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and\n\\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing\n(\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its import closure inside the repository is\n\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one with the prefix\n\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}, totalling \\textbf{550,551} raw\nlines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}). The\nclosure stops at \\textbf{50} Mathlib modules and one module of Lean's own\nlibrary, \\texttt{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega}, imported by\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean} (line~14) and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean} (line~5); it\nis a tactic module, and the closure defines no tactic, macro or syntax of\nits own (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}). Nothing is imported from any\nother family of the collection or from any of the repository's other\ndependencies.\n\nThe family's directory holds \\textbf{3,977} \\texttt{.lean} files, so\n\\textbf{886} are outside the closure. They are not reachable from the\ncollection's root module either: \\clonefile{lean/OAI.lean} imports the\nfamily only at its line~49, \\texttt{import\nOAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, and the lakefile's default target\nis that root (\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~89,\n\\texttt{@[default\\_target] lean\\_lib OAI}). They are therefore never built\nby the default target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For\ncompleteness, a raw search of the 886 for \\texttt{sorry}, \\texttt{axiom},\n\\texttt{native\\_decide} and \\texttt{admit} finds nothing, with or without\ncomments.\n\n\\subsection{The keyword sweep}\n\\label{sec:proof-keywords}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection} gives the counts over the 3,091 files\nwith comments stripped. The ones that matter are the zeros. There is no\n\\texttt{axiom}, no \\texttt{sorry}, no \\texttt{admit}; no\n\\texttt{native\\_decide} and no \\texttt{ofReduceBool}, so nothing is\nproved by trusting the compiled evaluator; no \\texttt{implemented\\_by},\n\\texttt{extern}, \\texttt{unsafe} or \\texttt{partial}, so no definition\nhas a hidden implementation and every recursion is one the kernel checks\n(nine \\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses);\nno \\texttt{elab}, \\texttt{macro}, \\texttt{syntax} or \\texttt{simproc}, so\nthere is no metaprogramming that could rewrite goals outside the kernel's\nview. The only \\texttt{set\\_option}s are two\n\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),\nwhich switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9).\n\nNine declarations are \\texttt{opaque}. Every one carries a body: three\nrecovery functions (\\texttt{queryRecovery},\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean} line~59 with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery},\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean} lines 178 and\n198) and six \\texttt{private opaque} constants of subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = …\\}} with an explicit value\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} line~22 and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding\nis hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the\ntype, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}\ndeclarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit\nuses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the\nclassical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s\nand 40 \\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype},\n\\texttt{Decidable} and algebraic instances; an instance cannot be\n``inconsistent'' without an axiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines\nwith \\texttt{decide} are kernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the\nnative evaluator.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The inspection in numbers. Comments stripped where a keyword is\ncounted. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof\\_inspection.py}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-inspection}\n\\input{tables/proof_inspection}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The statement is the challenge's}\n\\label{sec:proof-match}\n\nThe solution file does not import the challenge file:\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins (lines 1--3) with\n\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement} and\n\\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names,\nspread over seven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the\nbit arithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding\nfunctions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in\n\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the\nspace instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and\n\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}\n(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares\n\\textbf{35} declarations: \\textbf{33} are identical character for\ncharacter after whitespace normalisation, and the remaining two,\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} and \\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the\nchallenge writes \\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15)---a difference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement\nitself, from \\texttt{theorem exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by},\nis \\textbf{identical} in the two files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124\nafter normalisation, at line~194 of the challenge and line~74 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the challenge's proof is the single\nword \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\n\nThis textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The\ncomparator \\citep{LeanComparator} compares the two statements at the\nlevel of exported constants, with every declaration the statement\nmentions matched, so a library definition that differed from the\nchallenge's under the same name would make the comparator fail rather\nthan pass silently. What the text confirms is that there is no such\ndifference to find, and that the shape Section~\\ref{sec:statement} read\nis the shape the library proves.\n\n\\subsection{The spine of the proof}\n\\label{sec:proof-spine}\n\n\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is\n\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape\n\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k (NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing, and it is the\nreason no running time can be read off the statement\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\n\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},\n\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},\nlines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with \\texttt{1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A}\n(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where\n\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract\n(\\texttt{NativeLeafContract.uniform\\_contract}, line~39; the contract\ngives \\texttt{1 ≤ At}, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\n(\\texttt{Filter.eventually\\_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$\nfactor is assembled by \\texttt{runtime\\_of\\_tree\\_and\\_leaf} (line~46)\nfrom the DFS visit bound of \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most\na square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)\nand the leaf time. For \\texttt{n ≤ cutoff},\n\\texttt{Native.entry\\_small\\_primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives\n\\texttt{time ≤ 28700 · (L + 1)²} (line~92) through the finite scanner\nof \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are\nfolded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and\n\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: \\texttt{db +\n(4 · (k − 2) + 2) + df + 2}) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what\nSection~\\ref{sec:statement} said from the statement side: the constants\nare unknown, and the \\emph{smallest} they could be is already far from\n$1$.\n\n\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}\nA proof of a running-time bound for a machine it compiles from a\nhigher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound against a cost\nfunction of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function's\narithmetic. That is not what happens here. The compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive\\_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\texttt{CompileSpace.compile\\_word\\_answer\\_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time …} with\n\\texttt{time ≤ 7 * sourceSteps} and\n\\texttt{visitedSpace intermediate ≤ s * (bound + input.length + 15)} on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\texttt{primitive\\_located\\_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are\nthe challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and\n\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its\nline~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;\na wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem\nfalse. This is the structural point on which the rest of the paper\nleans: whatever the proof's internal accounting, the number the theorem\nbounds is the number of steps of the comparator's machine.\n\n\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and\n\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\texttt{Native.counting\\_primitive\\_from\\_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement. These are theorems about the concrete step function.\nThe arithmetic that makes them true---sparsification, the ring and\nmoment computations, the DFS---is the content of the other three\nthousand files, and the only claim made about it here is the one the\nkernel makes: it elaborates (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks}\n\\label{sec:proof-build}\n\nThe toolchain is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:\n\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}\nline~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest lists 42\npackages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at\n\\texttt{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05\nconfirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that bumps the\ntoolchain to \\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility\npatches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/}, \\textbf{none for Mathlib};\nevery one is for a third-party package (\\texttt{AINTLIB},\n\\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd}, \\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, …) that\nthis closure does not import. The lakefile has two hooks that run\narbitrary IO when Lake configures or updates the package: a\n\\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that evaluates dependency\ndeclarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck} and calls\n\\texttt{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches eleven\ndependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward) that\nverifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only\n\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages; they are one\nreason the inspection did not build, and the reason the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay} runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n\\subsection{What the repository does not record}\n\\label{sec:proof-missing}\n\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a\nforty-year-old hypothesis are absent, and their absence is a fact about\nevidence rather than about the proof.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No axiom audit.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} ends\n  with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and seven\n  other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen commands in\n  eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such files, but\n  nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output exists only\n  while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of the whole\n  clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{No continuous integration.} \\clonefile{.github/workflows/}\n  holds one file, \\texttt{lock-prs.yml}, which locks pull-request\n  conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\\item \\textbf{No second kernel for this family.} The comparator's JSON\n  (\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}) names the\n  one theorem, an empty \\texttt{definition\\_names}, the three permitted\n  axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice},\n  and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}; the registry\n  \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines 1683--1685) points at the\n  same declaration and file. Of the collection's 418 challenge JSONs,\n  413 set \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda} to \\texttt{false}, two to\n  \\texttt{true}, and three omit the key. With it off, the collection's own\n  check of this family rests on one kernel implementation. The\n  comparator's README, as fetched by the audit, describes the check as:\n  build challenge and solution in a sandbox, export both with\n  \\texttt{lean4export}, match each named theorem's statement with every\n  declaration it uses, confine the proof to the permitted axioms, and\n  have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on, the exported\n  environment is replayed through an independent kernel in Rust as well\n  \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the versions.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nTwo smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source is\ndated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''\n(\\clonefile{preprints/…/build/main\\_full\\_version.tex}, line~25); the\ncollection's history file mentions the family once, in the sentence that\nnames the working group at whose request the materials were released\n(\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only ``Formalization Fix''\nentry (line~7) concerns another family. And the scope note\n\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as\n$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library\nproves and what Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} compares with the manuscript's.\n\n\\subsection{The trust boundary, joined with the replay}\n\\label{sec:proof-trust}\n\nThe audit's verdict listed what the proof's trust rested on before\nanything was run: (i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed\nthe closure type-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output;\n(iii) the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7}\nas upstream; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no second\nkernel for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which of the\nfive Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The trust boundary before and after the replay of\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}.}\n\\label{tab:proof-trust}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\\linewidth}p{0.64\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\nitem & status after the replay \\\\\n\\midrule\n(i) the build & \\textbf{moved}: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox at the pinned toolchain, exit~0, no \\texttt{error}, no \\texttt{sorry}. \\\\\n(ii) the axiom output & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the comparator's theorem printed \\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, and the nineteen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record. \\\\\n(iii) kernel and Mathlib & \\textbf{moved in part}: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned revision and not patched, so ``as upstream'' is now observed rather than assumed; the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} is still trusted as upstream, now beside a second kernel (v). \\\\\n(iv) the comparator run & \\textbf{not moved}: the statement-level match through \\texttt{lean4export} was not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof-match}. \\\\\n(v) a second kernel & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} checked the exported dependencies of the theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms; what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on: the Lean kernel of\n\\texttt{v4.34.1} \\emph{and} nanoda agreeing on the exported dependencies\nof the theorem; \\texttt{lean4export} exporting what the environment\nholds; upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not\nexport-level, match between the solution's statement and the\nchallenge's. Everything the inspection could find by reading, the build\nand the two kernels confirmed; nothing the inspection could not settle\nby reading has been settled by anything else.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:proof-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{The arithmetic was not read.} The inspection followed the\n  spine from \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} down to the compile\n  lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the sparsification, ring\n  and DFS content of the other three thousand files was not read. The\n  argument above is that its correctness is kernel-enforced, not that we\n  verified it.\n\\item \\textbf{Grep is necessary, not sufficient.} Zero \\texttt{sorry}\n  and zero \\texttt{axiom} by text search cannot show that a file\n  elaborates; only the build does (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Conversely\n  a keyword count is over source text at one commit, and a declaration\n  can be unsound only through the mechanisms searched for; we searched\n  for the ones we know.\n\\item \\textbf{The textual statement match is not the comparator's.} The\n  comparator matches exported constants; we matched source text after\n  whitespace normalisation and found it identical. A difference the\n  normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well,\n  but that is an argument, not a run.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the constants' values.} The bounds\n  $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ are what the proof's witnesses are\n  built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n  statements about any machine's running time, and a different proof of\n  the same statement could exhibit different ones.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain.}\n  All three are trusted as upstream at the pinned versions; the second\n  kernel of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is evidence about the first, not a\n  proof of either.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the theorem says.} That is\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}; a sound proof of a weak bound is a sound\n  proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":415,"lines":["-% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04.","+% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04 (2026-11-05). Every","+% number of this section is printed, and every line citation re-checked","+% against the read-only clone at commit 4eaee875, by","+%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py","+% which re-runs the two audit scripts of reports/scratch/eth_audit/","+% (closure.py, defdiff.py) as subprocesses, performs the remaining checks","+% itself, writes latex/tables/proof_inspection.tex, and exits non-zero if","+% anything has moved. Unqualified Lean paths below are relative to","+% lean/OAI/Algorithms/SubexponentialSAT/ in the clone; the challenge file is","+% lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean. Nothing in the clone was","+% built, imported or run for this section; the build is Section 5's."," \\section{The proof, inspected}"," \\label{sec:proof}"," ","-\\placeholder{Item 04 drafts this section: the closure size, the zero counts, the 33 identical definitions, the compile lemmas concluding in the comparator's own \\texttt{Trace}, the unpatched Mathlib, the absence of a recorded axiom audit, of CI and of a second kernel; joined with the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. Ends with what this does not claim''.}","+Section~\\ref{sec:statement} read the theorem; this section reads the","+proof, as far as reading can go. The question is not whether the","+mathematics is right---the kernel decides that, and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}","+reports what the kernel said when we ran it---but whether anything in the","+source could make the kernel's acceptance mean less than it appears to","+mean: an axiom, a \\texttt{sorry}, a trusted evaluation, an unsound","+instance, a patched Mathlib, a statement that differs from the challenge's,","+or a cost model of the proof's own that leaks into the theorem. We found","+none of these. What we did find is that the repository offers no recorded","+evidence that the closure type-checks at all---no \\texttt{\\#print axioms}","+output, no continuous integration, no second kernel for this family---which","+is why Section~\\ref{sec:replay} exists. The last subsection joins the two.","+","+\\subsection{Method}","+\\label{sec:proof-method}","+","+The clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated as","+untrusted data: every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},","+\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,","+imported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of","+2026-11-05 do the mechanical part: \\texttt{closure.py} follows","+\\texttt{import} lines from the solution module, strips comments (nested","+block comments included) and counts keyword occurrences in what remains;","+\\texttt{defdiff.py} normalises whitespace and compares each declaration of","+the challenge file with the library's declaration of the same name. Both","+were re-run for this section, and every number below is from that fresh","+run; \\repofile{paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py} re-runs them, performs","+the remaining checks, and regenerates Table~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection}.","+The inspection is of the spine of the proof---the final file, the","+compiler, the entry lemmas, the constants---and of the whole closure by","+keyword. The arithmetic beneath the spine was not read","+(Section~\\ref{sec:proof-not}).","+","+\\subsection{The closure}","+\\label{sec:proof-closure}","+","+The comparator's JSON names one solution module,","+\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, a sixteen-line aggregator","+that imports \\texttt{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and","+\\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing","+(\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its import closure inside the repository is","+\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one with the prefix","+\\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}, totalling \\textbf{550,551} raw","+lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}). The","+closure stops at \\textbf{50} Mathlib modules and one module of Lean's own","+library, \\texttt{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega}, imported by","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean} (line~14) and","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean} (line~5); it","+is a tactic module, and the closure defines no tactic, macro or syntax of","+its own (Section~\\ref{sec:proof-keywords}). Nothing is imported from any","+other family of the collection or from any of the repository's other","+dependencies.","+","+The family's directory holds \\textbf{3,977} \\texttt{.lean} files, so","+\\textbf{886} are outside the closure. They are not reachable from the","+collection's root module either: \\clonefile{lean/OAI.lean} imports the","+family only at its line~49, \\texttt{import","+OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, and the lakefile's default target","+is that root (\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~89,","+\\texttt{@[default\\_target] lean\\_lib OAI}). They are therefore never built","+by the default target and bear on nothing: dead files, not a hole. For","+completeness, a raw search of the 886 for \\texttt{sorry}, \\texttt{axiom},","+\\texttt{native\\_decide} and \\texttt{admit} finds nothing, with or without","+comments.","+","+\\subsection{The keyword sweep}","+\\label{sec:proof-keywords}","+","+Table~\\ref{tab:proof-inspection} gives the counts over the 3,091 files","+with comments stripped. The ones that matter are the zeros. There is no","+\\texttt{axiom}, no \\texttt{sorry}, no \\texttt{admit}; no","+\\texttt{native\\_decide} and no \\texttt{ofReduceBool}, so nothing is","+proved by trusting the compiled evaluator; no \\texttt{implemented\\_by},","+\\texttt{extern}, \\texttt{unsafe} or \\texttt{partial}, so no definition","+has a hidden implementation and every recursion is one the kernel checks","+(nine \\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses);","+no \\texttt{elab}, \\texttt{macro}, \\texttt{syntax} or \\texttt{simproc}, so","+there is no metaprogramming that could rewrite goals outside the kernel's","+view. The only \\texttt{set\\_option}s are two","+\\texttt{set\\_option quotPrecheck false}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, line~15, and","+\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}, line~14),","+which switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on","+soundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or","+\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile","+sets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package","+(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9).","+","+Nine declarations are \\texttt{opaque}. Every one carries a body: three","+recovery functions (\\texttt{queryRecovery},","+\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean} line~59 with its","+\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and","+\\texttt{fixedRecovery},","+\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean} lines 178 and","+198) and six \\texttt{private opaque} constants of subtype","+\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = …\\}} with an explicit value","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} line~22 and five","+others). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body is a definition whose unfolding","+is hidden from elaboration; the kernel still checks the body against the","+type, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}","+declarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical}, and the four explicit","+uses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the","+classical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s","+and 40 \\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype},","+\\texttt{Decidable} and algebraic instances; an instance cannot be","+``inconsistent'' without an axiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines","+with \\texttt{decide} are kernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the","+native evaluator.","+","+\\begin{table}[t]","+\\centering\\small","+\\caption{The inspection in numbers. Comments stripped where a keyword is","+counted. Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/proof\\_inspection.py}.}","+\\label{tab:proof-inspection}","+\\input{tables/proof_inspection}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\subsection{The statement is the challenge's}","+\\label{sec:proof-match}","+","+The solution file does not import the challenge file:","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} begins (lines 1--3) with","+\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry}, \\texttt{SqrtLogSpace.Statement} and","+\\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every","+definition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names,","+spread over seven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the","+bit arithmetic in \\clonefile{BinaryArithmetic.lean}, the nine encoding","+functions in \\clonefile{Encoding.lean}, the machine in","+\\clonefile{Computation.lean}, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and","+\\texttt{runtimeBound} in \\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, the","+space instrumentation in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/VisitedTape.lean} and","+\\texttt{countWord} in \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/EncodingBounds.lean}","+(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares","+\\textbf{35} declarations: \\textbf{33} are identical character for","+character after whitespace normalisation, and the remaining two,","+\\texttt{runtimeScale} and \\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the","+challenge writes \\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a","+\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},","+line~15)---a difference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement","+itself, from \\texttt{theorem exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by},","+is \\textbf{identical} in the two files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124","+after normalisation, at line~194 of the challenge and line~74 of","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the challenge's proof is the single","+word \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.","+","+This textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The","+comparator \\citep{LeanComparator} compares the two statements at the","+level of exported constants, with every declaration the statement","+mentions matched, so a library definition that differed from the","+challenge's under the same name would make the comparator fail rather","+than pass silently. What the text confirms is that there is no such","+difference to find, and that the shape Section~\\ref{sec:statement} read","+is the shape the library proves.","+","+\\subsection{The spine of the proof}","+\\label{sec:proof-spine}","+","+\\paragraph{The machine.} The theorem's machine is","+\\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive program} with the count tape","+\\texttt{256} and the answer tape \\texttt{1216} (both \\texttt{: Fin 1220};","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}","+is \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k (NativeLeafProgram.command k)}","+(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a","+first-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and","+a finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds","+(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a","+\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table","+\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the","+stack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the","+stack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to","+\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).","+The machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the","+program, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is","+existential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing, and it is the","+reason no running time can be read off the statement","+(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).","+","+\\paragraph{The time bound, and the constants.} For \\texttt{cutoff < n},","+\\texttt{NativeLargeEntry.run} (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean},","+lines 11--33) supplies a trace of length at most","+\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n} (line~27) with \\texttt{1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A}","+(line~12). Its exponent constant is \\texttt{256 + At} (line~51), where","+\\texttt{At} comes from the leaf program's uniform contract","+(\\texttt{NativeLeafContract.uniform\\_contract}, line~39; the contract","+gives \\texttt{1 ≤ At}, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},","+line~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),","+with \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality","+(\\texttt{Filter.eventually\\_atTop}, line~48). The $2^{A\\cdot\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\,n}$","+factor is assembled by \\texttt{runtime\\_of\\_tree\\_and\\_leaf} (line~46)","+from the DFS visit bound of \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 53--56: visits at most","+a square polynomial in $n$ times $2^{n/\\mathtt{sparsifySchedule}\\,n}$)","+and the leaf time. For \\texttt{n ≤ cutoff},","+\\texttt{Native.entry\\_small\\_primitive}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.lean}, lines 81--100) gives","+\\texttt{time ≤ 28700 · (L + 1)²} (line~92) through the finite scanner","+of \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean}. The two branches are","+folded by \\texttt{runtime\\_with\\_fallback} and","+\\texttt{space\\_with\\_fallback}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)","+into one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are","+\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written","+has an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a","+polynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: \\texttt{db +","+(4 · (k − 2) + 2) + df + 2}) and a leading constant of at least","+$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that","+they are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is","+computed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what","+Section~\\ref{sec:statement} said from the statement side: the constants","+are unknown, and the \\emph{smallest} they could be is already far from","+$1$.","+","+\\paragraph{Where a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot.}","+A proof of a running-time bound for a machine it compiles from a","+higher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound against a cost","+function of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function's","+arithmetic. That is not what happens here. The compile lemmas conclude in","+the comparator's own vocabulary: \\texttt{GuardedCompiler.primitive\\_execution}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a","+source run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step","+(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem","+(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and","+\\texttt{CompileSpace.compile\\_word\\_answer\\_space}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a","+\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time …} with","+\\texttt{time ≤ 7 * sourceSteps} and","+\\texttt{visitedSpace intermediate ≤ s * (bound + input.length + 15)} on","+every prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\texttt{primitive\\_located\\_step}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step","+form. \\texttt{Trace}, \\texttt{locatedStep} and \\texttt{visitedSpace} are","+the challenge's definitions (its lines 87--90 and 149--189), and","+\\texttt{time} in the final theorem is literally the length of a","+\\texttt{Computation.Trace} of \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.step} (its","+line~201). No cost function of the compiler's appears in the statement;","+a wrong cost model would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem","+false. This is the structural point on which the rest of the paper","+leans: whatever the proof's internal accounting, the number the theorem","+bounds is the number of steps of the comparator's machine.","+","+\\paragraph{Correctness of the count.} \\texttt{Native.outer\\_from\\_setup}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean}, lines 50--51) yields","+\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.countStack = countWord (SAT.count F)} and","+\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from","+the canonical DFS execution, and","+\\texttt{Native.counting\\_primitive\\_from\\_entry} (line~73) transports","+both to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and","+\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses","+of the statement. These are theorems about the concrete step function.","+The arithmetic that makes them true---sparsification, the ring and","+moment computations, the DFS---is the content of the other three","+thousand files, and the only claim made about it here is the one the","+kernel makes: it elaborates (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).","+","+\\subsection{Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks}","+\\label{sec:proof-build}","+","+The toolchain is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:","+\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}","+line~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest lists 42","+packages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision","+\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at","+\\texttt{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05","+confirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that bumps the","+toolchain to \\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility","+patches under \\clonefile{lean/patches/}, \\textbf{none for Mathlib};","+every one is for a third-party package (\\texttt{AINTLIB},","+\\texttt{PrimeNumberTheoremAnd}, \\texttt{carleson}, \\texttt{iut}, …) that","+this closure does not import. The lakefile has two hooks that run","+arbitrary IO when Lake configures or updates the package: a","+\\texttt{run\\_cmd} block (lines 290--304) that evaluates dependency","+declarations with \\texttt{unsafe env.evalConstCheck} and calls","+\\texttt{preparePatchedDependencies}, which clones and patches eleven","+dependencies, and a \\texttt{post\\_update} hook (line~306 onward) that","+verifies or applies the patches for the rest. They touch only","+\\texttt{.lake/packages}, and only non-Mathlib packages; they are one","+reason the inspection did not build, and the reason the replay of","+Section~\\ref{sec:replay} runs inside a sandbox with the network on.","+","+\\subsection{What the repository does not record}","+\\label{sec:proof-missing}","+","+Three things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a","+forty-year-old hypothesis are absent, and their absence is a fact about","+evidence rather than about the proof.","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{No axiom audit.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} ends","+  with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and seven","+  other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen commands in","+  eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such files, but","+  nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output exists only","+  while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of the whole","+  clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds nothing.","+\\item \\textbf{No continuous integration.} \\clonefile{.github/workflows/}","+  holds one file, \\texttt{lock-prs.yml}, which locks pull-request","+  conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.","+\\item \\textbf{No second kernel for this family.} The comparator's JSON","+  (\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}) names the","+  one theorem, an empty \\texttt{definition\\_names}, the three permitted","+  axioms \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice},","+  and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}; the registry","+  \\clonefile{lean/formalization.yaml} (lines 1683--1685) points at the","+  same declaration and file. Of the collection's 418 challenge JSONs,","+  413 set \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda} to \\texttt{false}, two to","+  \\texttt{true}, and three omit the key. With it off, the collection's own","+  check of this family rests on one kernel implementation. The","+  comparator's README, as fetched by the audit, describes the check as:","+  build challenge and solution in a sandbox, export both with","+  \\texttt{lean4export}, match each named theorem's statement with every","+  declaration it uses, confine the proof to the permitted axioms, and","+  have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on, the exported","+  environment is replayed through an independent kernel in Rust as well","+  \\citep{Nanoda}. Section~\\ref{sec:related} records the versions.","+\\end{itemize}","+","+Two smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source is","+dated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''","+(\\clonefile{preprints/…/build/main\\_full\\_version.tex}, line~25); the","+collection's history file mentions the family once, in the sentence that","+names the working group at whose request the materials were released","+(\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only ``Formalization Fix''","+entry (line~7) concerns another family. And the scope note","+\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as","+$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library","+proves and what Section~\\ref{sec:beyond} compares with the manuscript's.","+","+\\subsection{The trust boundary, joined with the replay}","+\\label{sec:proof-trust}","+","+The audit's verdict listed what the proof's trust rested on before","+anything was run: (i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed","+the closure type-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output;","+(iii) the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7}","+as upstream; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no second","+kernel for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which of the","+five Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.","+","+\\begin{table}[t]","+\\centering\\small","+\\caption{The trust boundary before and after the replay of","+Section~\\ref{sec:replay}.}","+\\label{tab:proof-trust}","+\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\\linewidth}p{0.64\\linewidth}@{}}","+\\toprule","+item & status after the replay \\\\","+\\midrule","+(i) the build & \\textbf{moved}: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox at the pinned toolchain, exit~0, no \\texttt{error}, no \\texttt{sorry}. \\\\","+(ii) the axiom output & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the comparator's theorem printed \\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, and the nineteen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record. \\\\","+(iii) kernel and Mathlib & \\textbf{moved in part}: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned revision and not patched, so ``as upstream'' is now observed rather than assumed; the Lean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} is still trusted as upstream, now beside a second kernel (v). \\\\","+(iv) the comparator run & \\textbf{not moved}: the statement-level match through \\texttt{lean4export} was not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof-match}. \\\\","+(v) a second kernel & \\textbf{moved}: \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} checked the exported dependencies of the theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms; what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. \\\\","+\\bottomrule","+\\end{tabular}","+\\end{table}","+","+After the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on: the Lean kernel of","+\\texttt{v4.34.1} \\emph{and} nanoda agreeing on the exported dependencies","+of the theorem; \\texttt{lean4export} exporting what the environment","+holds; upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not","+export-level, match between the solution's statement and the","+challenge's. Everything the inspection could find by reading, the build","+and the two kernels confirmed; nothing the inspection could not settle","+by reading has been settled by anything else.","+","+\\subsection{What this does not claim}","+\\label{sec:proof-not}","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{The arithmetic was not read.} The inspection followed the","+  spine from \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean} down to the compile","+  lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the sparsification, ring","+  and DFS content of the other three thousand files was not read. The","+  argument above is that its correctness is kernel-enforced, not that we","+  verified it.","+\\item \\textbf{Grep is necessary, not sufficient.} Zero \\texttt{sorry}","+  and zero \\texttt{axiom} by text search cannot show that a file","+  elaborates; only the build does (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Conversely","+  a keyword count is over source text at one commit, and a declaration","+  can be unsound only through the mechanisms searched for; we searched","+  for the ones we know.","+\\item \\textbf{The textual statement match is not the comparator's.} The","+  comparator matches exported constants; we matched source text after","+  whitespace normalisation and found it identical. A difference the","+  normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well,","+  but that is an argument, not a run.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the constants' values.} The bounds","+  $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$ are what the proof's witnesses are","+  built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not","+  statements about any machine's running time, and a different proof of","+  the same statement could exhibit different ones.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain.}","+  All three are trusted as upstream at the pinned versions; the second","+  kernel of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is evidence about the first, not a","+  proof of either.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the theorem says.} That is","+  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}; a sound proof of a weak bound is a sound","+  proof of a weak bound.","+\\end{itemize}"]}],"originalFile":"% The proof, inspected -- drafted by loop0015 item 04.\n\\section{The proof, inspected}\n\\label{sec:proof}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 04 drafts this section: the closure size, the zero counts, the 33 identical definitions, the compile lemmas concluding in the comparator's own \\texttt{Trace}, the unpatched Mathlib, the absence of a recorded axiom audit, of CI and of a second kernel; joined with the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01L4MBXLCE1Hbo9tLp53f6Sn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
